 # Registry Design
 
-## Context
+## Context and Design Philosophy
 
 Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
 
-## Architecture
+The registry owns snapshot validation, index preparation, and serial-number lookup. The lookup interface `findBySerial(serial)` returns `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)` with record fields and result types fixed for the certificates node. The certificates component must not read registry storage directly.
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+## Snapshot Import
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Verify the signature using the bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate that serial numbers are unique within the snapshot. Validation and index build run against a staging copy. Activation swaps the active snapshot atomically only after all validation and index build succeed. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
 
-## Snapshot Import
+With the local SQLite index, import reads the newline-delimited JSON records, creates a temporary SQLite database in memory or a staging file, builds an indexed table keyed by normalized serial number, and copies the snapshot metadata. The active database file is replaced atomically on success. The previous database remains usable for lookups during import.
+
+## Lookup
+
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+Lookup queries the embedded SQLite index on the kiosk. The index is rebuilt entirely when a new snapshot is activated. Lookups complete using only files on the kiosk; no runtime network access is permitted.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; Managed search service | Predictable lookups with no service to run; each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on snapshot arrival, keeping all index maintenance inside the registry component. Satisfies the tenet of no runtime network access and keeps certificate interface unchanged. |
+| Index rebuild strategy | Full rebuild on snapshot activation | Incremental delta updates | Simpler correctness with signed snapshots; snapshots are whole replacements, and a full rebuild guarantees index consistency with the snapshot date. |
+
